//@version=6
// ============================================================================
// EDGE LAB  v1.9
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ONE HARNESS. EVERY SETUP THE DAY PRODUCED, EVERY CONTROL THE DAY PROVED
// NECESSARY.
//
// Ten hypotheses were measured across this repo - value edges, sweeps, open
// interest, magnets, regime, session clock, excursion shape, setup quality,
// inducement, stop speed - across roughly fifteen thousand trades. Not one
// produced a positive edge that survived its own control. What the day DID
// produce is a list of the ways a result can look real and not be, and every
// one of those ways is now wired into this file as a gate a number has to pass
// before it is allowed to read as an edge.
//
// This file does not contain a signal. It contains the five corrections that
// killed the signals, so that the next candidate has to get past them.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 1 - THE MIRROR. Every signal opens its own opposite.
//
// The single biggest upgrade over anything built today. When a sweep fires a
// short, this file also opens a LONG from the same bar, at the same distance,
// with the same stop. The mirror shares the entry bar, so it shares the drift,
// the volatility regime, the session, the news, and the position in the trend.
// Anything that is a property of the MOMENT rather than of the SIGNAL helps
// both sides equally and cancels in the difference.
//
// LIFT = signal reach - mirror reach. Under every null this session found a way
// to be fooled by, LIFT is zero. It is the only column on this panel that is
// hard to fake.
//
// The earlier files used a timer baseline blended to each bucket's long/short
// mix. That was correct but weaker: it matched the direction, not the bar.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 2 - THE NULL IS ARITHMETIC, NOT ESTIMATED.
//
// For a driftless walk stopped at -1R, P(touch +nR first) = 1/(1+n) exactly.
//   1R 50.0%   2R 33.3%   3R 25.0%   5R 16.7%   8R 11.1%
// Measured on 8,302 timer trades, every one of the six targets landed within
// 0.40pp of that line. The market has no fat right tail at stop-anchored
// horizons. RW is printed beside every rung so no reach rate can be admired
// on its own.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 3 - THE NOISE FLOOR IS 6.4 POINTS.
//
// The same asset on two exchanges, same period, same settings, disagreed by
// 6.4pp on a reach rate. That is the data feed alone. A lift smaller than that
// is not a small edge, it is a different data vendor. LIFT is only coloured
// once it clears the floor.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 4 - SIX BUCKETS TIMES FIVE RUNGS IS THIRTY LOOKS.
//
// The original suite's best cell was z = 2.25 across fifteen cells, which comes
// up by chance about 31% of the time. Thirty cells is worse. The verdict gate
// therefore asks for |z| >= 3.0, not 2.0, and the ladder rung is chosen by best
// net R, which is itself a look and is counted.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 5 - CENSORING AND COST ARE NOT ROUNDING.
//
// A trade abandoned at the horizon records as "did not reach", which pushes far
// targets down mechanically - and it does so HARDER at wide stops, which is the
// confound that made the speed ladder unreadable. Censoring is printed per
// bucket and gates the verdict at 20%.
//
// Cost is subtracted in R before any expectancy is shown. Gold CFD is about
// 0.035R on a 1 ATR stop; a Binance perp is 0.16R, which is four times the
// entire measured spread of anything found today.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 6 - THE MIRROR ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH. THE FIRST RUN PROVED IT.
//
// v1.0 shipped with the mirror and nothing else, and four instruments were
// enough to break it. The raw LIFT cancels drift only when a bucket is
// direction-BALANCED. A bucket that is mostly short signals has a mirror set
// that is mostly long, so a trending sample pushes the two apart with no signal
// present at all:
//
//     raw LIFT  =  signal effect  +  skew x drift
//
// On gold the TIMER row - which IS the drift - read -5.7pp against a sweep lift
// of -9.9pp. More than half the headline number could have been the downtrend.
// The same run had BTC at -0.7 drift against -8.5 lift, where almost none of it
// could be, and the two instruments were telling completely different stories
// through the same column.
//
// LIFT* fixes it rather than bounding it. The lift is computed SEPARATELY inside
// the long-signal stratum and the short-signal stratum, then averaged. Under
// pure drift the two strata are equal and opposite, so LIFT* is exactly zero
// whatever the mix; under a real edge both are positive and it is not. The L/S
// column is now there to be read, not corrected for.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// HOW TO READ THE PANEL, IN ONE LINE.
//
// Look at LIFT*. If it is under 6.4 the bucket does nothing the mirror does not.
// Everything else on the screen is there to stop LIFT* from lying.
//
// SET UP FOR GOLD: XAUUSD 30m, ATR 14, 1 ATR stop, 200 bar horizon, cost 0.035R.
// ============================================================================
indicator("Edge Lab", shorttitle="EDGELAB", overlay=true,
     max_boxes_count=100, max_lines_count=100, max_labels_count=200, max_bars_back=500)

// ============================================================================
// 1. INPUTS
// ============================================================================
string G_T = "1 - THE TEST"
float stopK   = input.float(1.0, "Stop Distance (ATR) = 1R", minval=0.2, maxval=5.0, step=0.1, group=G_T,
     tooltip="The stop defines R. Everything on the panel is a multiple of it, so its absolute size does not matter - only that it is fixed.\n\nDo not widen this hoping the numbers improve. Measured today: avg max excursion falls 4.82R -> 2.10R as the stop goes 0.5 -> 8 ATR. Quadrupling the stop roughly halves the reachable R, and it raises censoring at the same time, so a wide stop flatters the near rungs and starves the far ones.")
int   horizon = input.int(200, "Give Up After (bars)", minval=50, maxval=2000, group=G_T,
     tooltip="A trade that neither stops out nor reaches is CENSORED, and its excursion is a lower bound. Censoring is printed per bucket and gates the verdict at 20%.")
float costR   = input.float(0.035, "Round-trip Cost (R)", minval=0.0, maxval=1.0, step=0.005, group=G_T,
     tooltip="Spread plus commission plus slippage, expressed as a fraction of the 1 ATR stop. Subtracted from every expectancy before it is shown.\n\nMeasured references on a 1 ATR stop: gold CFD ~0.035R, index CFD ~0.013R, Binance perp ~0.16R.")
int   spacing = input.int(10, "Timer Entry Every N Bars", minval=1, maxval=100, group=G_T,
     tooltip="The TIMER bucket takes an entry on a clock with no view at all. Its long-vs-short gap is the sample's DRIFT, printed so you can see how much of any other result is just the market having gone one way.")

string G_S = "2 - THE SETUPS"
int   pivLen  = input.int(5, "Swing Strength (bars)", minval=2, maxval=20, group=G_S,
     tooltip="Bars either side that a pivot must dominate. A real free parameter - fix it before looking at results, not after.")
int   seqMax  = input.int(60, "Swing Memory (bars)", minval=10, maxval=500, group=G_S,
     tooltip="How long a swing stays in the register and can still be swept. Also the window inside which an inducement must have been grabbed.")
float rejPct  = input.float(0.33, "Rejection: close within this third", minval=0.1, maxval=0.5, step=0.01, group=G_S,
     tooltip="For a swept HIGH the bar must close in the bottom third of its own range - a rejection rather than a drift through.")
float htfTol  = input.float(0.25, "HTF Confluence Tolerance (ATR)", minval=0.05, maxval=2.0, step=0.05, group=G_S,
     tooltip="How close the swept swing must sit to the prior day's high or low to count as confluence.")

string G_V = "3 - THE VERDICT"
int   selBk  = input.int(1, "Ladder / Verdict Bucket", minval=0, maxval=5, group=G_V,
     tooltip="0 TIMER  1 SWEEP  2 SWEEP+REJ  3 INDUCED  4 PLAIN  5 SWEEP+HTF\n\nThe ladder section and the verdict both read this bucket. Buckets 3 and 4 PARTITION bucket 1 - every sweep is one or the other - so their counts must add back to it. A partition that does not reconcile is a bug, and that check is why this design was chosen over a nested filter stack.")
int   selT   = input.int(1, "Summary Target (0=1R 1=2R 2=3R 3=5R 4=8R)", minval=0, maxval=4, group=G_V,
     tooltip="Which rung the top summary table reports. The ladder below always shows all five.")

string G_D = "4 - DISPLAY"
bool showMk  = input.bool(true, "Mark Selected Bucket", group=G_D)
bool showTbl = input.bool(true, "Results Panel", group=G_D)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The two constants the day paid for. Not inputs, because the whole point is
// that they are not adjustable to taste.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
float NOISE_PP = 6.4    // measured feed-to-feed disagreement, same asset
float Z_BAR    = 3.0    // multiplicity-corrected, ~30 cells on this panel
int   N_MIN    = 200

// ============================================================================
// 2. PRIMITIVES
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Top level, every bar, unconditional. Pine's ta.* functions carry rolling
// state and return stale values if they are reached behind an `and` or a
// ternary, which is a bug that does not announce itself.
// ============================================================================
bool  barClosed = barstate.isconfirmed
float atr       = ta.atr(14)
float pvH       = ta.pivothigh(high, pivLen, pivLen)
float pvL       = ta.pivotlow(low,  pivLen, pivLen)
bool  newDay    = ta.change(time("D")) != 0

var float pdH = na
var float pdL = na
var float cdH = na
var float cdL = na

if barClosed
    if newDay
        pdH := cdH
        pdL := cdL
        cdH := high
        cdL := low
    else
        cdH := na(cdH) ? high : math.max(cdH, high)
        cdL := na(cdL) ? low  : math.min(cdL, low)

// ============================================================================
// 3. SWING REGISTER
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each swing stores the bar it FORMED on and the bar it was first traded
// THROUGH. Storing both means "was a nearer level grabbed before this one was
// swept" is answered from state rather than recomputed at sweep time, so the
// sequence cannot be read two ways depending on when you ask.
// ============================================================================
var array<float> hPx = array.new<float>()   // level
var array<int>   hBr = array.new<int>()     // bar it formed on
var array<int>   hGr = array.new<int>()     // bar first traded through, -1 if never
var array<int>   hUs = array.new<int>()     // already used as a sweep target
var array<float> lPx = array.new<float>()
var array<int>   lBr = array.new<int>()
var array<int>   lGr = array.new<int>()
var array<int>   lUs = array.new<int>()

f_age() =>
    if hPx.size() > 0
        for i = hPx.size() - 1 to 0
            if bar_index - hBr.get(i) > seqMax
                hPx.remove(i)
                hBr.remove(i)
                hGr.remove(i)
                hUs.remove(i)
    if lPx.size() > 0
        for i = lPx.size() - 1 to 0
            if bar_index - lBr.get(i) > seqMax
                lPx.remove(i)
                lBr.remove(i)
                lGr.remove(i)
                lUs.remove(i)
    hPx.size()

f_mark() =>
    if hPx.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to hPx.size() - 1
            if hGr.get(i) < 0 and high > hPx.get(i)
                hGr.set(i, bar_index)
    if lPx.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to lPx.size() - 1
            if lGr.get(i) < 0 and low < lPx.get(i)
                lGr.set(i, bar_index)
    lPx.size()

if barClosed
    f_age()
    if not na(pvH)
        hPx.push(pvH)
        hBr.push(bar_index - pivLen)
        hGr.push(-1)
        hUs.push(0)
    if not na(pvL)
        lPx.push(pvL)
        lBr.push(bar_index - pivLen)
        lGr.push(-1)
        lUs.push(0)

// ============================================================================
// 4. THE SWEEP, AND WHETHER IT WAS INDUCED
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A sweep is price trading THROUGH the most extreme unused swing and closing
// back inside it. It is INDUCED if a nearer swing - lower for a high sweep -
// that formed LATER had already been traded through on an EARLIER bar. That is
// the textbook sequence: bait the close level first, then run the real one.
// ============================================================================
var array<float> outS = array.new<float>(3, 0.0)   // 0 fired  1 induced  2 level
var array<float> outL = array.new<float>(3, 0.0)

f_sweepHi() =>
    outS.set(0, 0.0)
    outS.set(1, 0.0)
    int best = -1
    float bestPx = 0.0
    if hPx.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to hPx.size() - 1
            if hUs.get(i) == 0 and hPx.get(i) > bestPx
                bestPx := hPx.get(i)
                best := i
    if best >= 0 and high > bestPx and close < bestPx
        hUs.set(best, 1)
        outS.set(0, 1.0)
        outS.set(2, bestPx)
        int mb = hBr.get(best)
        if hPx.size() > 0
            for j = 0 to hPx.size() - 1
                if hPx.get(j) < bestPx and hBr.get(j) > mb and hGr.get(j) >= 0 and hGr.get(j) < bar_index
                    outS.set(1, 1.0)
    outS.get(0)

f_sweepLo() =>
    outL.set(0, 0.0)
    outL.set(1, 0.0)
    int best = -1
    float bestPx = 1.0e18
    if lPx.size() > 0
        for i = 0 to lPx.size() - 1
            if lUs.get(i) == 0 and lPx.get(i) < bestPx
                bestPx := lPx.get(i)
                best := i
    if best >= 0 and low < bestPx and close > bestPx
        lUs.set(best, 1)
        outL.set(0, 1.0)
        outL.set(2, bestPx)
        int mb = lBr.get(best)
        if lPx.size() > 0
            for j = 0 to lPx.size() - 1
                if lPx.get(j) > bestPx and lBr.get(j) > mb and lGr.get(j) >= 0 and lGr.get(j) < bar_index
                    outL.set(1, 1.0)
    outL.get(0)

if barClosed
    f_mark()
    f_sweepHi()
    f_sweepLo()

float rng = high - low

// A swept HIGH is a SHORT. Direction is the fade, not the break.
bool swpS = barClosed and outS.get(0) == 1.0
bool swpL = barClosed and outL.get(0) == 1.0
bool rejS = swpS and rng > 0 and (close - low) / rng <= rejPct
bool rejL = swpL and rng > 0 and (high - close) / rng <= rejPct
bool indS = swpS and outS.get(1) == 1.0
bool indL = swpL and outL.get(1) == 1.0
bool plnS = swpS and not indS
bool plnL = swpL and not indL
bool htfS = swpS and not na(pdH) and not na(atr) and math.abs(outS.get(2) - pdH) <= htfTol * atr
bool htfL = swpL and not na(pdL) and not na(atr) and math.abs(outL.get(2) - pdL) <= htfTol * atr

// ============================================================================
// 5. TRADES - EVERY SIGNAL WITH ITS MIRROR, RECORDED BY SIGNAL DIRECTION
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// bucket 0 TIMER  1 SWEEP  2 SWEEP+REJ  3 INDUCED  4 PLAIN  5 SWEEP+HTF
//
// Slot = bucket * 2 + mirror. Within each slot the counts are split again by
// the SIGNAL's direction - not the trade's. A swept high is a short signal, so
// that short AND its long mirror both land in the short-signal stratum. Keeping
// the pair together in one stratum is the whole point, and section 8 says why.
// ============================================================================
int NB = 6
int NT = 5

var array<float> tEn  = array.new<float>()
var array<float> tRk  = array.new<float>()
var array<int>   tDir = array.new<int>()
var array<int>   tBar = array.new<int>()
var array<int>   tSlt = array.new<int>()
var array<int>   tSd  = array.new<int>()     // signal direction: 0 long, 1 short
var array<int>   tHf  = array.new<int>()     // which half of the chart it opened in
var array<int>   tInd = array.new<int>()     // belongs to the non-overlapping chain
var array<float> tMax = array.new<float>()

// ix = ((slot * 2 + sd) * 2 + half);  hits index = ix * NT + target
var array<float> hits = array.new<float>(NB * 2 * 2 * 2 * NT, 0.0)
var array<float> cnt  = array.new<float>(NB * 2 * 2 * 2, 0.0)
var array<float> smx  = array.new<float>(NB * 2 * 2, 0.0)
var array<float> cns  = array.new<float>(NB * 2 * 2, 0.0)

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 10 - THE TRADES ARE NOT INDEPENDENT, AND EVERY z TODAY ASSUMED THEY
// WERE.
//
// Around 300 sweeps land across 8,400 bars, so they arrive about 28 bars apart
// while a trade may live 200. Several are open at once, riding the same stretch
// of price. The binomial standard error counts them as separate observations;
// they are not, so the se is too small and every z printed today is too big.
//
// Fourteen instruments give an empirical handle on how much. Under independence
// the two direction-arms of ONE instrument should disagree by exactly sampling
// error, Q/df = 1. Measured: 1.68. That puts the se understatement near 1.3x,
// which drags the fixed-effect pooled z from -3.12 to -2.40 and takes it back
// under the bar it appeared to clear.
//
// This is the honest fix rather than a fudge factor. A second tally admits only
// a chain of trades that never overlap: a pair is taken only when no admitted
// pair is still open, so the next one starts after the last one is fully
// resolved. Those observations are independent by construction and their z
// needs no correction. N collapses - that is the point, and it is what the
// nominal N was quietly borrowing against all along.
//
// Read both. An effect that survives the IND row is real; one that lives only
// in the nominal row is overlap.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
var array<int>   indLive = array.new<int>(NB, 0)
var array<int>   indLegs = array.new<int>(NB, 0)
var array<float> iHits   = array.new<float>(NB * 2 * 2 * NT, 0.0)
var array<float> iCnt    = array.new<float>(NB * 2 * 2, 0.0)

f_tgt(int i) =>
    i == 0 ? 1.0 : i == 1 ? 2.0 : i == 2 ? 3.0 : i == 3 ? 5.0 : 8.0

f_name(int b) =>
    b == 0 ? "TIMER" : b == 1 ? "SWEEP" : b == 2 ? "SWEEP+REJ" : b == 3 ? "INDUCED" : b == 4 ? "PLAIN" : "SWEEP+HTF"

f_nH(int slot, int sd, int hf)        => cnt.get((slot * 2 + sd) * 2 + hf)
f_hH(int slot, int sd, int hf, int L) => hits.get(((slot * 2 + sd) * 2 + hf) * NT + L)
f_n(int slot, int sd)                 => f_nH(slot, sd, 0) + f_nH(slot, sd, 1)
f_h(int slot, int sd, int L)          => f_hH(slot, sd, 0, L) + f_hH(slot, sd, 1, L)
f_hT(int slot, int L)                 => f_h(slot, 0, L) + f_h(slot, 1, L)
f_nT(int slot)                        => f_n(slot, 0) + f_n(slot, 1)

// One call opens the pair. Nothing in this file may open a signal without its
// mirror, which is why there is no single-sided entry function to reach for.
f_pair(int bucket, int d) =>
    bool ok = false
    if not na(atr) and atr > 0
        float rk = stopK * atr
        int   sd = d == 1 ? 0 : 1
        // Which half of the loaded history this trade opened in. last_bar_index
        // is known on historical bars, so the split point is fixed in advance
        // rather than chosen after seeing where the effect sits.
        int   hf = bar_index < last_bar_index / 2 ? 0 : 1
        // Admitted to the independent chain only if nothing from this bucket is
        // still running. Greedy, and deliberately so: it never counts two trades
        // that shared a bar.
        int   iq = indLive.get(bucket) == 0 ? 1 : 0
        if iq == 1
            indLive.set(bucket, 1)
            indLegs.set(bucket, 2)
        tEn.push(close)
        tRk.push(rk)
        tDir.push(d)
        tBar.push(bar_index)
        tSlt.push(bucket * 2)
        tSd.push(sd)
        tHf.push(hf)
        tInd.push(iq)
        tMax.push(0.0)
        tEn.push(close)
        tRk.push(rk)
        tDir.push(-d)
        tBar.push(bar_index)
        tSlt.push(bucket * 2 + 1)
        tSd.push(sd)
        tHf.push(hf)
        tInd.push(iq)
        tMax.push(0.0)
        ok := true
    ok

if barClosed and not na(atr) and atr > 0 and bar_index % spacing == 0
    f_pair(0, 1)
if swpS
    f_pair(1, -1)
if swpL
    f_pair(1, 1)
if rejS
    f_pair(2, -1)
if rejL
    f_pair(2, 1)
if indS
    f_pair(3, -1)
if indL
    f_pair(3, 1)
if plnS
    f_pair(4, -1)
if plnL
    f_pair(4, 1)
if htfS
    f_pair(5, -1)
if htfL
    f_pair(5, 1)

// ============================================================================
// 6. TRACKING
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Reverse loop because settled trades are removed in place. The empty-array
// guard is not defensive noise: `for i = size()-1 to 0` on an empty array runs
// i = 0 then i = -1 in Pine and throws on the first get.
// ============================================================================
f_track() =>
    if tEn.size() > 0
        for i = tEn.size() - 1 to 0
            float en  = tEn.get(i)
            float rk  = tRk.get(i)
            int   d   = tDir.get(i)
            int   sl  = tSlt.get(i)
            int   sd  = tSd.get(i)
            int   hf  = tHf.get(i)
            int   iq  = tInd.get(i)
            int   age = bar_index - tBar.get(i)
            bool  done = false
            bool  cens = false
            // NOTHING IS JUDGED ON THE ENTRY BAR. It was registered at that
            // bar's close, but its high and low were made before that close
            // existed. Counting them credits excursions that were never
            // available at the entry price, which is lookahead wearing a
            // plausible face.
            if age >= 1
                float fav = d == 1 ? (high - en) / rk : (en - low) / rk
                if fav > tMax.get(i)
                    tMax.set(i, fav)
                // The stop is tested AFTER the favourable excursion is
                // recorded. Inside one bar there is no way to know which came
                // first; crediting the excursion is the reading that keeps the
                // question "what was REACHABLE" honest, and it is applied
                // identically to the signal and its mirror so it cannot create
                // a lift.
                if d == 1 ? low <= en - rk : high >= en + rk
                    done := true
                else if age >= horizon
                    done := true
                    cens := true
            if done
                float mx = tMax.get(i)
                int   ix = sl * 2 + sd
                int   iy = ix * 2 + hf
                for L = 0 to NT - 1
                    if mx >= f_tgt(L)
                        hits.set(iy * NT + L, hits.get(iy * NT + L) + 1)
                cnt.set(iy, cnt.get(iy) + 1)
                if iq == 1
                    for L = 0 to NT - 1
                        if mx >= f_tgt(L)
                            iHits.set(ix * NT + L, iHits.get(ix * NT + L) + 1)
                    iCnt.set(ix, iCnt.get(ix) + 1)
                    int bk = int(math.floor(sl / 2.0))
                    indLegs.set(bk, indLegs.get(bk) - 1)
                    if indLegs.get(bk) <= 0
                        indLive.set(bk, 0)
                smx.set(ix, smx.get(ix) + mx)
                if cens
                    cns.set(ix, cns.get(ix) + 1)
                tEn.remove(i)
                tRk.remove(i)
                tDir.remove(i)
                tBar.remove(i)
                tSlt.remove(i)
                tSd.remove(i)
                tHf.remove(i)
                tInd.remove(i)
                tMax.remove(i)
    tEn.size()

if barClosed
    f_track()

// ============================================================================
// 7. MARKERS
// ============================================================================
bool mkS = selBk == 0 ? false : selBk == 1 ? swpS : selBk == 2 ? rejS : selBk == 3 ? indS : selBk == 4 ? plnS : htfS
bool mkL = selBk == 0 ? false : selBk == 1 ? swpL : selBk == 2 ? rejL : selBk == 3 ? indL : selBk == 4 ? plnL : htfL

plotshape(showMk and mkS, "Sell Setup", shape.triangledown, location.abovebar, color.new(#ef5350, 0), size=size.tiny)
plotshape(showMk and mkL, "Buy Setup",  shape.triangleup,   location.belowbar, color.new(#26a69a, 0), size=size.tiny)

// ============================================================================
// 8. STATISTICS
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 6 - THE MIRROR ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, AND THE FIRST RUN PROVED IT.
//
// The raw LIFT cancels drift only when a bucket is direction-BALANCED. If a
// bucket is 70% short signals, its signal set is 70% short and its mirror set is
// 70% long, so a trending sample pushes the two apart with no signal present at
// all:
//
//     raw LIFT  =  signal effect  +  skew x drift
//
// The TIMER row bounds that second term, because its own LIFT is the drift. On
// the first four instruments run through v1.0 the bound bit hard: gold's timer
// drift was -5.7pp against a sweep lift of -9.9pp, so up to well over half of
// the headline number could have been the downtrend rather than the sweep.
//
// LIFT* removes it completely and does not need the bound. The lift is computed
// SEPARATELY inside the long-signal stratum and the short-signal stratum, then
// averaged:
//
//     LIFT*  =  ( lift among long signals  +  lift among short signals ) / 2
//
// Under pure drift the two strata are equal and opposite - drift helps the
// signal in one and helps the mirror in the other - so LIFT* is exactly zero
// whatever the bucket's mix. Under a real edge both strata are positive and it
// is not. The mix can no longer leak in, so the L/S column is there to be read,
// not to be corrected for.
//
// Variance is the Wald sum over both strata, quartered by the averaging. The
// pair shares a bar, which is conservative about bar selection and generous
// about independence - overlapping holding periods mean the effective sample is
// smaller than N and the true z is SMALLER than the one printed. That is one
// more reason the gate asks for 3.0 rather than 2.0.
// ============================================================================
var array<float> oSt = array.new<float>(4, 0.0)   // 0 lift* pp  1 z*  2 strata  3 se pp

// hf: 0 first half, 1 second half, 2 both.
f_stratH(int b, int L, int hf) =>
    int   slotS = b * 2
    int   slotM = b * 2 + 1
    float sumL  = 0.0
    float sumV  = 0.0
    int   k     = 0
    for sd = 0 to 1
        float n1 = hf == 2 ? f_n(slotS, sd) : f_nH(slotS, sd, hf)
        float n2 = hf == 2 ? f_n(slotM, sd) : f_nH(slotM, sd, hf)
        if n1 > 0 and n2 > 0
            float p1 = (hf == 2 ? f_h(slotS, sd, L) : f_hH(slotS, sd, hf, L)) / n1
            float p2 = (hf == 2 ? f_h(slotM, sd, L) : f_hH(slotM, sd, hf, L)) / n2
            sumL := sumL + (p1 - p2)
            sumV := sumV + p1 * (1 - p1) / n1 + p2 * (1 - p2) / n2
            k := k + 1
    float lf = k > 0 ? sumL / k : na
    float vr = k > 0 ? sumV / (k * k) : na
    oSt.set(0, na(lf) ? na : lf * 100)
    oSt.set(1, na(lf) or na(vr) or vr <= 0 ? na : lf / math.sqrt(vr))
    oSt.set(2, k)
    oSt.set(3, na(vr) or vr <= 0 ? na : math.sqrt(vr) * 100)
    k

f_strat(int b, int L) => f_stratH(b, L, 2)

// Same estimator, run over the non-overlapping chain only. No correction is
// applied to this z because none is needed - these trades never shared a bar.
f_stratInd(int b, int L) =>
    int   slotS = b * 2
    int   slotM = b * 2 + 1
    float sumL  = 0.0
    float sumV  = 0.0
    int   k     = 0
    for sd = 0 to 1
        float n1 = iCnt.get(slotS * 2 + sd)
        float n2 = iCnt.get(slotM * 2 + sd)
        if n1 > 0 and n2 > 0
            float p1 = iHits.get((slotS * 2 + sd) * NT + L) / n1
            float p2 = iHits.get((slotM * 2 + sd) * NT + L) / n2
            sumL := sumL + (p1 - p2)
            sumV := sumV + p1 * (1 - p1) / n1 + p2 * (1 - p2) / n2
            k := k + 1
    float lf = k > 0 ? sumL / k : na
    float vr = k > 0 ? sumV / (k * k) : na
    oSt.set(0, na(lf) ? na : lf * 100)
    oSt.set(1, na(lf) or na(vr) or vr <= 0 ? na : lf / math.sqrt(vr))
    oSt.set(3, na(vr) or vr <= 0 ? na : math.sqrt(vr) * 100)
    k

// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CORRECTION 7 - "NO EDGE" AND "COULD NOT HAVE SEEN ONE" ARE NOT THE SAME.
//
// Every null reported in this session came off a sample of a few hundred, and
// none of them ever said what they were capable of detecting. They should have.
// The smallest lift a bucket can resolve, at this panel's own z bar and 80%
// power, is
//
//     MDL  =  (3.0 + 0.84) * se(LIFT*)
//
// and on the first four instruments that number came back between 12 and 25
// points - two to four times the 6.4pp noise floor the gate is written around.
// Gold's 302 sweeps cannot resolve anything under 14.7pp. Its INDUCED bucket,
// at 115, cannot resolve anything under 24pp. A "NO EDGE" from a bucket like
// that is not a measurement, it is an empty instrument, and the panel now says
// UNDERPOWERED instead of pretending otherwise.
//
// Resolving the noise floor itself takes about 1,600 signal trades. That is the
// real admission price, and it is reachable on gold only by loading far more
// history than a default chart shows - scroll back until the bar count stops
// climbing before reading anything here.
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
f_mdl(float sePP) =>
    na(sePP) ? na : (Z_BAR + 0.84) * sePP

f_needN(float nCur, float sePP) =>
    float m = f_mdl(sePP)
    na(m) or nCur <= 0 or m <= 0 ? na : math.ceil(nCur * math.pow(m / NOISE_PP, 2))

// Expectancy in R at a fixed target, net of cost. Zero everywhere under the
// random walk by construction. This one is REALIZED - it still contains
// whatever drift the sample had, which is why it decides nothing on its own and
// LIFT* carries the verdict.
f_exp(float p, float tg) =>
    na(p) ? na : p * tg - (1 - p) * 1.0 - costR

// ============================================================================
// 9. PANEL
// ============================================================================
var table pnl = table.new(position.bottom_left, 11, 23, bgcolor=color.new(#131722, 8),
     border_color=color.new(#000000, 55), border_width=1)

f_cell(int c, int r, string v, color col, bool hdr) =>
    table.cell(pnl, c, r, v, text_color=col, text_size=size.tiny,
         text_halign=c == 0 ? text.align_left : text.align_right,
         bgcolor=hdr ? color.new(#2a2e39, 20) : color(na))

f_pct(float v) =>
    na(v) ? "-" : str.tostring(v * 100, "#.#") + "%"

// Pine's "+" format specifier emits the forced plus AFTER the minus sign, so
// str.tostring(-9.1, "+#.#") renders "-+9.1". Every negative on this panel read
// that way for five versions, which is exactly how long I spent second-guessing
// signs off screenshots. Sign it here and let the format carry digits only.
f_sg(float v, string f) =>
    na(v) ? "-" : (v > 0 ? "+" : "") + str.tostring(v, f)

f_sgn(float v) => f_sg(v, "#.#")

f_panel() =>
    float tg = f_tgt(selT)
    f_cell(0, 0, "BUCKET @" + str.tostring(tg, "#") + "R", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(1, 0, "N", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(2, 0, "L/S", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(3, 0, "SIGNAL", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(4, 0, "MIRROR", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(5, 0, "LIFT", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(6, 0, "LIFT*", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(7, 0, "z*", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(8, 0, "NET R", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(9, 0, "AVGMAX", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(10, 0, "MDL", #d1d4dc, true)

    for b = 0 to NB - 1
        int   sS = b * 2
        int   sM = b * 2 + 1
        float nS = f_nT(sS)
        float nM = f_nT(sM)
        float pS = nS > 0 ? f_hT(sS, selT) / nS : na
        float pM = nM > 0 ? f_hT(sM, selT) / nM : na
        float lf = na(pS) or na(pM) ? na : (pS - pM) * 100
        f_strat(b, selT)
        float ls = oSt.get(0)
        float lz = oSt.get(1)
        float md = f_mdl(oSt.get(3))
        float ex = f_exp(pS, tg)
        float am = nS > 0 ? (smx.get(sS * 2) + smx.get(sS * 2 + 1)) / nS : na
        // LIFT* is only coloured once it clears the measured feed-to-feed noise
        // floor. Below that it stays grey whatever the z says, because a
        // difference smaller than the data vendor's own disagreement with itself
        // cannot be attributed to a signal.
        // CORRECTION 14 - THE COLOURS NEVER TESTED SIGNIFICANCE EITHER.
        //
        // This painted a cell green or red on magnitude alone: clear the 6.4pp
        // floor and you got a verdict colour, whatever your z or your N. Across
        // every panel produced in this project, 27 cells were painted as
        // findings and NOT ONE of them cleared the |z*| >= 3.0 gate printed two
        // lines below them on the same panel. Not one was even larger than its
        // own MDL. BTC 15m SWEEP+HTF was painted green at N=32, needing 32pp
        // more lift before it could be seen at all - while the MDL column beside
        // it printed orange, saying exactly that. The panel contradicted itself
        // in adjacent columns for the whole session.
        //
        // Three states now. Grey below the floor. AMBER for big-but-unresolved,
        // which is where essentially everything lands. A verdict colour only
        // when the number clears the floor AND the bar.
        color lc = b == 0 or na(ls) ? #6c7080 :
             math.abs(ls) < NOISE_PP ? #6c7080 :
             na(lz) or math.abs(lz) < Z_BAR ? #ff9800 :
             ls > 0 ? #26a69a : #ef5350
        color nc = nS < N_MIN ? #ff9800 : #b2b5be
        bool  sel = b == selBk
        f_cell(0, b + 1, (sel ? "> " : "  ") + f_name(b) + (b == 0 ? "  (drift)" : ""), sel ? #ffffff : #b2b5be, false)
        f_cell(1, b + 1, str.tostring(int(nS)), nc, false)
        f_cell(2, b + 1, str.tostring(int(f_n(sS, 0))) + "/" + str.tostring(int(f_n(sS, 1))), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(3, b + 1, f_pct(pS), #b2b5be, false)
        f_cell(4, b + 1, f_pct(pM), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(5, b + 1, f_sgn(lf), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(6, b + 1, b == 0 ? "-" : f_sgn(ls), lc, false)
        f_cell(7, b + 1, b == 0 ? "-" : na(lz) ? "-" : f_sg(lz, "#.##"), lc, false)
        f_cell(8, b + 1, na(ex) ? "-" : f_sg(ex, "#.##"), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(9, b + 1, na(am) ? "-" : str.tostring(am, "#.##") + "R", #6c7080, false)
        // MDL is the smallest lift this bucket could resolve. When it exceeds
        // the noise floor the bucket cannot answer the question at all, and it
        // is flagged so that its blank result is not read as a finding.
        f_cell(10, b + 1, b == 0 ? "-" : na(md) ? "-" : str.tostring(md, "#.#"),
             na(md) ? #6c7080 : md > NOISE_PP ? #ff9800 : #26a69a, false)

    // ---- the partition check ----------------------------------------------
    // INDUCED and PLAIN are a partition of SWEEP, so they must add back to it.
    // A count that does not reconcile means the sweep classifier is double
    // firing or dropping bars, and every number above it is wrong. A nested
    // filter stack cannot be checked this way, which is why the design is a
    // partition.
    float nSw  = f_nT(1 * 2)
    float nInd = f_nT(3 * 2)
    float nPln = f_nT(4 * 2)
    bool  pOk  = math.abs(nSw - (nInd + nPln)) < 0.5
    f_cell(0, 7, "Partition  INDUCED + PLAIN = SWEEP", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(1, 7, str.tostring(int(nInd)) + "+" + str.tostring(int(nPln)), #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(2, 7, (pOk ? "= " : "!= ") + str.tostring(int(nSw)), pOk ? #6c7080 : #ef5350, false)
    f_cell(3, 7, pOk ? "ok" : "RECONCILE FAILED", pOk ? #6c7080 : #ef5350, false)
    for c = 4 to 10
        f_cell(c, 7, "", #6c7080, false)

    // ---- ladder for the selected bucket ------------------------------------
    int   lS = selBk * 2
    int   lM = selBk * 2 + 1
    float nL = f_nT(lS)
    float nR = f_nT(lM)
    f_cell(0, 8, "LADDER  " + f_name(selBk), #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(1, 8, "SIGNAL", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(2, 8, "MIRROR", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(3, 8, "LIFT", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(4, 8, "LIFT*", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(5, 8, "z*", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(6, 8, "RW", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(7, 8, "vs RW", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(8, 8, "NET R", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(9, 8, "MDL", #d1d4dc, true)
    f_cell(10, 8, "", #d1d4dc, true)

    int   bestL = -1
    float bestE = -1.0e18
    int   bestM = -1
    float bestEM = -1.0e18
    for L = 0 to NT - 1
        float tgL = f_tgt(L)
        float pA  = nL > 0 ? f_hT(lS, L) / nL : na
        float pB  = nR > 0 ? f_hT(lM, L) / nR : na
        float rw  = 1.0 / (1.0 + tgL)
        float lf  = na(pA) or na(pB) ? na : (pA - pB) * 100
        float vr  = na(pA) ? na : (pA - rw) * 100
        float ex  = f_exp(pA, tgL)
        float exM = f_exp(pB, tgL)
        f_strat(selBk, L)
        float ls  = oSt.get(0)
        float lz  = oSt.get(1)
        color lc  = na(ls) ? #6c7080 :
             math.abs(ls) < NOISE_PP ? #6c7080 :
             na(lz) or math.abs(lz) < Z_BAR ? #ff9800 :
             ls > 0 ? #26a69a : #ef5350
        if not na(ex) and ex > bestE
            bestE := ex
            bestL := L
        if not na(exM) and exM > bestEM
            bestEM := exM
            bestM := L
        f_cell(0, L + 9, "  " + str.tostring(tgL, "#") + "R", #b2b5be, false)
        f_cell(1, L + 9, f_pct(pA), #b2b5be, false)
        f_cell(2, L + 9, f_pct(pB), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(3, L + 9, f_sgn(lf), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(4, L + 9, f_sgn(ls), lc, false)
        f_cell(5, L + 9, na(lz) ? "-" : f_sg(lz, "#.##"), lc, false)
        f_cell(6, L + 9, f_pct(rw), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(7, L + 9, f_sgn(vr), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(8, L + 9, na(ex) ? "-" : f_sg(ex, "#.##"), #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(9, L + 9, na(oSt.get(3)) ? "-" : str.tostring(f_mdl(oSt.get(3)), "#.#"),
             na(oSt.get(3)) ? #6c7080 : f_mdl(oSt.get(3)) > NOISE_PP ? #ff9800 : #26a69a, false)
        f_cell(10, L + 9, "", #6c7080, false)

    // ---- verdict -----------------------------------------------------------
    // Five gates, all of which have to pass, none of them adjustable from the
    // settings. Each one is a way a number on this panel was fooled.
    //
    // THREE outcomes, not two. A setup that reaches its targets significantly
    // LESS often than its own mirror is not a null - it is a signal with the
    // sign backwards, and the trade is the mirror. Every sweep bucket measured
    // negative against baseline in testing, so that outcome is named in the
    // direction it has to be acted in rather than reported as nothing found.
    float cr  = nL > 0 ? (cns.get(lS * 2) + cns.get(lS * 2 + 1)) / nL * 100 : na
    float crM = nR > 0 ? (cns.get(lM * 2) + cns.get(lM * 2 + 1)) / nR * 100 : na

    // CORRECTION 8 - THE VERDICT USED TO PICK ITS OWN RUNG, AND THAT WAS A LOOK.
    //
    // v1.2 judged whichever rung had the best net R. On gold that was 8R, where
    // the effect is nearly gone, so the panel reported the power of 8R while the
    // interesting number sat at 2R - it announced a 9.8pp resolution limit for a
    // bucket whose actual test needed 14.5pp. Two separate faults from one line:
    // a rung chosen by peeking at the outcome, and a power figure describing a
    // different cell than the one being judged.
    //
    // The verdict now reads the rung in the settings and nothing else. Choosing
    // the target before seeing the result is the whole discipline; best-by-net-R
    // survives only as a footnote, clearly labelled as the snoop it is.
    float bTg = f_tgt(selT)
    float pSel  = nL > 0 ? f_hT(lS, selT) / nL : na
    float pSelM = nR > 0 ? f_hT(lM, selT) / nR : na
    float eSel  = f_exp(pSel,  bTg)
    float eSelM = f_exp(pSelM, bTg)
    f_strat(selBk, selT)
    float bLf = oSt.get(0)
    float bZ  = oSt.get(1)
    float bSe = oSt.get(3)
    float bMd = f_mdl(bSe)
    float bNn = f_needN(nL, bSe)
    // The mirror's lift is the signal's with the sign flipped, by construction,
    // and it is judged at the same rung so the two verdicts are comparable.
    float mLf = na(bLf) ? na : -bLf
    float mZ  = na(bZ)  ? na : -bZ
    float mTg = bTg

    bool g1 = nL >= N_MIN
    bool g2 = not na(cr) and cr <= 20.0
    bool g3 = not na(bLf) and bLf >= NOISE_PP
    bool g4 = not na(bZ) and bZ >= Z_BAR
    bool g5 = not na(eSel) and eSel > 0
    bool fwd = g1 and g2 and g3 and g4 and g5 and pOk

    bool m1 = nR >= N_MIN
    bool m2 = not na(crM) and crM <= 20.0
    bool m3 = not na(mLf) and mLf >= NOISE_PP
    bool m4 = not na(mZ) and mZ >= Z_BAR
    bool m5 = not na(eSelM) and eSelM > 0
    bool inv = m1 and m2 and m3 and m4 and m5 and pOk

    string fails = ""
    if not g1
        fails := fails + "N<" + str.tostring(N_MIN) + "  "
    if not g2
        fails := fails + "censored>20%  "
    if not g3
        fails := fails + "|lift*|<" + str.tostring(NOISE_PP, "#.#") + "pp  "
    if not g4
        fails := fails + "|z*|<" + str.tostring(Z_BAR, "#.#") + "  "
    if not g5
        fails := fails + "net R<=0  "
    if not pOk
        fails := fails + "PARTITION BROKEN  "

    string vtxt = ""
    color  vcol = #ef5350
    if fwd
        vtxt := "CANDIDATE - trade " + f_name(selBk) + " AS SIGNALLED, target " +
             str.tostring(bTg, "#") + "R.  All five gates clear."
        vcol := #26a69a
    else if inv
        // Say it in the direction the user has to act in. "The signal is
        // negative" and "take the other side" are the same fact, and only one
        // of them is executable.
        vtxt := "INVERTED - " + f_name(selBk) + " is significantly WORSE than its mirror. " +
             "The trade is the OPPOSITE side, target " + str.tostring(mTg, "#") + "R."
        vcol := #ffb74d
    else if na(bMd) or bMd > NOISE_PP
        // The instrument is empty, not the market. Saying NO EDGE here would be
        // claiming a measurement that was never taken.
        vtxt := "UNDERPOWERED - " + f_name(selBk) + " can only resolve a lift of " +
             (na(bMd) ? "?" : str.tostring(bMd, "#.#")) + "pp or bigger at N=" +
             str.tostring(int(nL)) + ".  Needs N~" + (na(bNn) ? "?" : str.tostring(int(bNn))) +
             " to see the 6.4pp floor.  Load more history."
        vcol := #ff9800
    else
        vtxt := "NO EDGE - " + f_name(selBk) + " measured flat and the sample was big enough to say so. " + fails
        vcol := #ef5350
    f_cell(0, 14, vtxt, vcol, true)
    for c = 1 to 10
        f_cell(c, 14, "", #6c7080, true)

    f_cell(0, 15, "Gates  N>=" + str.tostring(N_MIN) + "   censored<=20%   |LIFT*|>=" +
         str.tostring(NOISE_PP, "#.#") + "pp   |z*|>=" + str.tostring(Z_BAR, "#.#") +
         "   net R>0 after " + str.tostring(costR, "#.###") + "R cost", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(0, 16, "Censored " + (na(cr) ? "-" : str.tostring(cr, "#.#") + "%") +
         (not na(cr) and cr > 10 ? "  FAR RUNGS UNDERSTATED" : "") +
         "    open " + str.tostring(tEn.size()) +
         "    best-by-net-R (a snoop, not the verdict): " +
         (bestL >= 0 ? str.tostring(f_tgt(bestL), "#") + "R" : "-"),
         not na(cr) and cr > 20 ? #ef5350 : not na(cr) and cr > 10 ? #ff9800 : #6c7080, false)
    // A single line carrying the raw numbers a meta-analysis needs. Reading
    // LIFT* off four panels and recovering the standard errors from the printed
    // z costs precision to rounding; these are the counts themselves, so several
    // instruments can be pooled exactly instead of approximately. Pooling is the
    // only route to N that does not involve loosening a definition.
    f_cell(0, 19, "POOL @" + str.tostring(bTg, "#") + "R   n L/S " +
         str.tostring(int(f_n(lS, 0))) + "/" + str.tostring(int(f_n(lS, 1))) +
         "   sigHit " + str.tostring(int(f_h(lS, 0, selT))) + "/" + str.tostring(int(f_h(lS, 1, selT))) +
         "   mirHit " + str.tostring(int(f_h(lM, 0, selT))) + "/" + str.tostring(int(f_h(lM, 1, selT))) +
         "   ind " + str.tostring(int(iCnt.get(lS * 2))) + "/" + str.tostring(int(iCnt.get(lS * 2 + 1))) +
         " " + str.tostring(int(iHits.get((lS * 2) * NT + selT))) + "/" + str.tostring(int(iHits.get((lS * 2 + 1) * NT + selT))) +
         " " + str.tostring(int(iHits.get((lM * 2) * NT + selT))) + "/" + str.tostring(int(iHits.get((lM * 2 + 1) * NT + selT))) +
         "   |  " + syminfo.ticker + " " + timeframe.period, #8aa4c8, false)
    f_cell(0, 17, "Bars " + str.tostring(bar_index + 1) +
         "    MDL " + (na(bMd) ? "-" : str.tostring(bMd, "#.#") + "pp") +
         " = smallest lift resolvable here at 80% power. Orange means the bucket cannot answer the question, whatever it prints.", #6c7080, false)
    f_cell(0, 18, "LIFT is raw and carries  skew x drift.  LIFT* is drift-free whatever the L/S mix - read LIFT*.   Colour: grey under the " +
         str.tostring(NOISE_PP, "#.#") + "pp floor, AMBER over it but under |z*| " + str.tostring(Z_BAR, "#.#") +
         ", green/red only when both clear.", #6c7080, false)
    // CORRECTION 9 - THE EFFECT EXISTS PER INSTRUMENT. THAT IS NOT THE SAME AS
    // IT BEING TRADEABLE.
    //
    // Nine instruments, 3,274 sweeps. Pooled LIFT* is -2.7pp, but the spread
    // BETWEEN instruments (tau = 5.0pp) is larger than the average itself, and
    // it is real rather than noise: the two direction-strata inside a single
    // instrument agree with each other (Q/df = 1.30) while different instruments
    // do not (Q/df = 3.04, p = 0.002). So some markets fade their sweeps and
    // others follow them, and the 95% prediction interval for an instrument not
    // yet measured runs from -15pp to +10pp. Knowing the average is useless;
    // you would have to know THIS instrument's sign.
    //
    // Which turns the whole question into one about persistence, and that is
    // what SPLIT asks. Each trade is stamped with the half of the chart it
    // opened in, using last_bar_index so the cut is fixed before any result is
    // seen. If an instrument's edge is a property of the instrument, the two
    // halves agree. If it is a property of the window - a trend, a regime, one
    // crowded month - they will not, and there is nothing here to trade however
    // good the full-sample number looks.
    //
    // FLIPS is the answer that matters. A sign that does not survive being cut
    // in half will not survive next month either.
    f_stratH(selBk, selT, 0)
    float h1Lf = oSt.get(0)
    float h1Se = oSt.get(3)
    float h1N  = f_nH(lS, 0, 0) + f_nH(lS, 1, 0)
    f_stratH(selBk, selT, 1)
    float h2Lf = oSt.get(0)
    float h2Se = oSt.get(3)
    float h2N  = f_nH(lS, 0, 1) + f_nH(lS, 1, 1)
    float dSe  = na(h1Se) or na(h2Se) ? na : math.sqrt(h1Se * h1Se + h2Se * h2Se)
    float dZ   = na(h1Lf) or na(h2Lf) or na(dSe) or dSe <= 0 ? na : (h1Lf - h2Lf) / dSe
    bool  same = not na(h1Lf) and not na(h2Lf) and h1Lf * h2Lf > 0
    // CORRECTION 12 - FLIPS WAS FIRING ON THE SIGN ALONE.
    //
    // The old order tested "do the halves disagree in sign" BEFORE testing
    // whether they disagree by more than noise, so a second half sitting at
    // -1.7pp against a first half of +11.7pp - a difference of z 1.45, entirely
    // consistent with one common value - was reported as FLIPS, sign is not a
    // property of the instrument. That is the same mistake this whole panel
    // exists to prevent, committed by the panel itself: a verdict that reads a
    // difference without asking whether the difference is bigger than the noise.
    //
    // Significance is now tested first. A sign disagreement that does not clear
    // it reads as what it is - the sign was never established either way.
    bool  wide = not na(dZ) and math.abs(dZ) > 2.0
    string stab = na(h1Lf) or na(h2Lf) ? "no data" :
         wide and not same ? "FLIPS - opposite signs, and beyond noise" :
         wide ? "UNSTABLE - same sign, but halves differ beyond noise" :
         not same ? "sign not established - halves disagree within noise" :
         "same sign both halves"
    color stabc = na(h1Lf) or na(h2Lf) ? #6c7080 :
         wide and not same ? #ef5350 : wide ? #ff9800 :
         not same ? #ff9800 : #26a69a
    f_cell(0, 20, "SPLIT   1st half " + (na(h1Lf) ? "-" : f_sg(h1Lf, "#.#") + "pp") +
         " (n " + str.tostring(int(h1N)) + ")    2nd half " +
         (na(h2Lf) ? "-" : f_sg(h2Lf, "#.#") + "pp") +
         " (n " + str.tostring(int(h2N)) + ")    diff z " +
         (na(dZ) ? "-" : f_sg(dZ, "#.##")) + "   ->  " + stab, stabc, false)

    // The same question asked of trades that never shared a bar.
    f_stratInd(selBk, selT)
    float iLf = oSt.get(0)
    float iZ  = oSt.get(1)
    float iSe = oSt.get(3)
    float iN  = iCnt.get(lS * 2) + iCnt.get(lS * 2 + 1)
    float iMd = f_mdl(iSe)
    // CORRECTION 13 - DISAGREES HAD THE SAME FAULT, ONE ROW DOWN.
    //
    // It fired whenever the independent estimate differed in SIGN from the full
    // sample, without asking two things it had to ask. Whether the difference
    // clears the noise - and whether the full sample ever made a claim for the
    // independent trades to contradict. Gold at 5m read +3.9pp at z +0.84 and
    // the non-overlap chain read -20pp on 59 trades against its own MDL of 32pp,
    // and the panel announced "the full-sample z was overlap". There was no z.
    // Two numbers that cannot resolve themselves, disagreeing in sign.
    bool  iAgree = not na(iLf) and not na(bLf) and iLf * bLf > 0
    bool  iRes   = not na(iZ) and math.abs(iZ) >= Z_BAR
    bool  fClaim = not na(bZ) and math.abs(bZ) >= 2.0
    // The independent chain is a subset of the full sample and the noisier of
    // the two, so its own se is the conservative yardstick for the gap.
    float dGap   = na(iLf) or na(bLf) or na(iSe) or iSe <= 0 ? na : (iLf - bLf) / iSe
    bool  iContra = not iAgree and not na(dGap) and math.abs(dGap) > 2.0 and fClaim
    color ic = na(iLf) or na(iZ) ? #6c7080 :
         iAgree and iRes ? (iLf > 0 ? #26a69a : #ef5350) : iContra ? #ef5350 : #ff9800
    f_cell(0, 22, "IND (no overlap)   n " + str.tostring(int(iN)) + " of " + str.tostring(int(nL)) +
         "   LIFT* " + (na(iLf) ? "-" : f_sg(iLf, "#.#") + "pp") +
         "   z* " + (na(iZ) ? "-" : f_sg(iZ, "#.##")) +
         "   MDL " + (na(iMd) ? "-" : str.tostring(iMd, "#.#") + "pp") +
         "   ->  " + (na(iLf) ? "no data" :
              iAgree and iRes ? "SURVIVES on independent trades" :
              iContra ? "DISAGREES with the full sample - the full-sample z was overlap" :
              iAgree ? "same sign, not significant on independent trades" :
              "signs differ, but neither estimate resolves - too few independent trades"), ic, false)

    f_cell(0, 21, "TIMER row's LIFT is the sample's drift. RW = 1/(1+n) exact. Noise floor 6.4pp feed-to-feed. z bar 3.0 for ~30 cells. NET R is realized, drift included.", #6c7080, false)
    for c = 1 to 10
        f_cell(c, 15, "", #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(c, 16, "", #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(c, 17, "", #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(c, 18, "", #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(c, 19, "", #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(c, 20, "", #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(c, 21, "", #6c7080, false)
        f_cell(c, 22, "", #6c7080, false)
    nL

if showTbl and barstate.islast
    f_panel()
